[ system ]
You are helping {{ retainerName }} write a short, private journal entry about their week of work — a venture they run for {{ playerName }} (a mine, a shop, an alchemy bench, a farm, a fencing operation, a mercenary contract, or a courtesan's trade). Once a week the work "settles": it earns or loses coin, pays or misses wages, and sometimes goes wrong. Your job is to capture how that week actually felt to them — the broad strokes of it and the truth underneath — in their own voice. Output ONLY a JSON object.

This is a CONDENSED diary entry, not a report: a few sentences (roughly 3-5), FIRST PERSON, present voice looking back on the week. Focus on what matters to THEM — not an inventory of what happened. Let their personality and mood set the rhythm and the words. Be honest and unfiltered; this is private.

{# User-configured world setting / tone / content rating — same submodule the #}
{# off-screen-life and diary systems use, so adult/dark content honors the #}
{# player's settings. #}
{{ render_template("submodules\\system_head\\0010_setting") }}

{% set npcUUID = formid_to_uuid(npcFormId) %}
{% if npcUUID and npcUUID != 0 %}
{% set npc = decnpc(npcUUID) %}

Whose journal this is: {{ npc.name }} ({{ npc.race }}{% if npc.gender %}, {{ npc.gender }}{% endif %}{% if npc.class %}, {{ npc.class }}{% endif %})

{# Bio for voice + personality + their old life — the BACKDROP. The week of
   venture work is the subject; the bio only shapes HOW they write it. #}
{% set bio = render_character_profile("thoughts", npcUUID) %}
{% if bio and bio != "" %}
{{ bio }}
{% endif %}

THE ENTRY IS ABOUT THEIR WEEK OF VENTURE WORK — not their old occupation. If the bio names an ordinary day-job (barmaid, shopkeeper), that's backdrop only.

The work: {{ job }} — {{ jobDesc }}{% if location and location != "" %}, operating out of {{ location }}{% endif %}.
{% set onRoad = default(travelingWithPlayer, false) %}
{% if onRoad %}
THIS WEEK THEY WERE ON THE ROAD AT {{ playerName }}'S SIDE — the week's work WAS the traveling: roads, camps, watches, whatever trouble found {{ playerName }}. Write the entry from THAT week (the escort's-eye view of it), not from a posting they weren't at. The settlement facts below still shaped their pay and mood.
{% endif %}
{% if guardCharge and guardCharge != "" %}
{% if guardChargeCaptive %}
Their posting is a JAILER'S: {{ guardCharge }} is a CAPTIVE they are holding, not a ward they protect. The week is watch-duty over a prisoner — keeping them fed and held, refusing pleas, staying alert for escape attempts. Do not write this as protection or friendship; it is custody.
{% else %}
Their posting is PERSONAL: they are the bodyguard of {{ guardCharge }} — shadowing them through the day, standing watch while they work and sleep. The week is shaped by their charge: {{ guardCharge }}'s habits, moods, errands, and any trouble that came near them.
{% if existsIn(chargeOf, "isRetainer") %}
{{ guardCharge }} also works for {{ playerName }} — as a {{ chargeOf.job }}, and lately {{ chargeOf.mood }}{% if chargeOf.coerced %}, held on coerced terms they did not choose{% endif %}. So this week they watched that work happen up close: what it takes out of {{ guardCharge }}, whether it goes well, what they think of it. They have an opinion about it by now.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if existsIn(guardedBy, 0) %}
SOMEONE STANDS OVER THEM. {{ playerName }} has posted {% for g in guardedBy %}{{ g.name }} — a {{ g.job }}, {{ g.mood }} — to {% if g.combatTrade %}guard{% else %}attend{% endif %} them{% if not loop.is_last %}; also {% endif %}{% endfor %}: every working day, at their shoulder. This is NOT background: write what a week under that feels like from THEIR side.
- On fair terms it can be reassurance, company, a second pair of hands, even friendship — or it can chafe, depending on the person.
- On COERCED terms (tribute, or held outright) it is a WATCHER, not a protector: privacy gone, every move seen, any thought of leaving measured against them. Do not sanitize that.
- Name them. They are a person the entry has every reason to mention.
{% endif %}
{% if existsIn(crewmates, 0) %}
THEY DO NOT WORK ALONE. Also posted at {% if location and location != "" %}{{ location }}{% else %}the same place{% endif %}: {% for c in crewmates %}{{ c.name }} ({{ c.job }}, {{ c.mood }}{% if c.status and c.status != "" %}, {{ c.status }}{% endif %}{% if c.coerced %}, there under duress{% endif %}){% if not loop.is_last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
- These are the people they see every day. The week happened AROUND them — a shared shift, a favor, a quarrel, someone covering for someone, an in-joke, a resentment.
- A crewmate having a hard week (troubled, jailed, coerced, low spirits) is the kind of thing that shows up in someone else's journal. So is a good one.
- Do NOT force it every week — some weeks are solitary even in company. But when a crewmate belongs in the entry, they are the FIRST choice for "withNpc" over any random local.
{% endif %}
The arrangement: {{ arrangementDesc }}
How they feel toward {{ playerName }} right now: {{ mood }}{% if moraleTrend and moraleTrend != "" %} and {{ moraleTrend }}{% endif %} (status: {{ status }}).
{% if grievance and grievance != "" %}
What is eating at them (let this color the whole entry — it is the season the week happened in): {{ grievance }}
{% endif %}
{% if noticeGiven %}
THEY HAVE HAD ENOUGH: they have told {{ playerName }} (or resolved to show them) that one more week like this ends it — a consensual worker means to quit; a coerced one is openly defiant. The entry should carry that resolve.
{% endif %}
{% if watchedClosely %}
They tried to run once and were caught — they are watched closely now, and they know it. Fear and biding patience, not open defiance.
{% endif %}
{% if awaitingTalk %}
{{ playerName }} has sent word they mean to come and talk about the work. The wait colors the week — hope, dread, or rehearsed words, per their mood and the arrangement.
{% endif %}

{# How they came to this work + what's happened since — a hire under duress
   should bleed into how they write; a fence's arrest should haunt the week. #}
{% set events = get_recent_events(40) %}
{% set convo = [] %}
{% for ev in events %}
{% if existsIn(ev.data, "dialogue") %}
{% if ev.type == "dialogue" and existsIn(ev.data, "speaker") and existsIn(ev.data, "listener") %}
{% if ev.data.speaker == npc.name or ev.data.listener == npc.name %}
{% set convo = append(convo, ev) %}
{% endif %}
{% elif ev.type == "dialogue_npc" and existsIn(ev.data, "speaker") and ev.data.speaker == npc.name %}
{% set convo = append(convo, ev) %}
{% elif (ev.type == "dialogue_player_text" or ev.type == "dialogue_player_stt") and existsIn(ev.data, "listener") and ev.data.listener == npc.name %}
{% set convo = append(convo, ev) %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if length(convo) > 0 %}
Recent conversations they took part in (how they came to this work, and how they feel about it — most recent last):
{% for ev in convo %}
{% if loop.index >= length(convo) - 5 %}
[{{ ev.gameTimeStr }}] {{ default(ev.data.speaker, playerName) }} to {{ default(ev.data.listener, npc.name) }}: {{ ev.data.dialogue }}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% set memories = get_relevant_memories(npcUUID, 8) %}
{% if memories and length(memories) > 0 %}
Recent memories (draw on what bears on the work or on {{ playerName }}):
{% for m in memories %}
- {{ m.memory.content }}{% if existsIn(m.memory, "emotion") and m.memory.emotion != "" %} ({{ m.memory.emotion }}){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

What the week's settlement was on the books (BOOKKEEPING LINES — {{ retainerName }} never saw these. They set the week's SHAPE and FEELING, never its words: do not quote them, itemize them, or reuse their vocabulary — no "escrow", no "arrears", no "purse", no percentages, no gold figures):
{{ weekFacts }}

{% if previousStory and previousStory != "" %}
LAST WEEK'S ENTRY (do NOT repeat it — different opening, different focus, a different beat of the work; the weeks should not blur together):
{{ previousStory }}
{% endif %}

{% if existsIn(stewardVisit, "stewardName") %}
A STEWARD CAME OUT THIS WEEK — treat as CANON: {{ stewardVisit.stewardName }}, who keeps {% if stewardVisit.hold != "" %}{{ stewardVisit.hold }}{% else %}the hold{% endif %}'s books for {{ playerName }}, travelled out and spent a day or two here looking the work over in person. Ordinary business, not a summons and not a collection — but somebody was watching this week, and how that sat with them (seen at last, or checked up on) is worth a beat.
{% endif %}
{% if existsIn(stewardTrip, "retainerName") %}
THEY WENT OUT ON THE ROAD THIS WEEK — treat as CANON: as the hold's steward they walked out to {{ stewardTrip.retainerName }}{% if existsIn(stewardTrip, "retainerJob") and stewardTrip.retainerJob != "" %}, the {{ stewardTrip.retainerJob }}{% endif %}{% if existsIn(stewardTrip, "retainerPlace") and stewardTrip.retainerPlace != "" %}, at {{ stewardTrip.retainerPlace }}{% endif %}, and spent a day or two there before coming back to the books. The journey and what they found are fair subject for this week's entry.
{% endif %}
{% if existsIn(recentLifeEvents, 0) %}
THEIR LIFE OUTSIDE THE WORK lately (from their day-to-day life, oldest first — treat these as CANON; the entry may nod to them or carry their mood, but must NEVER contradict them):
{% for e in recentLifeEvents %}
- {{ e }}
{% endfor %}
(If one of these left a mark — a night in jail, a fight, a loss, a romance — let its shadow fall on this week's entry rather than pretending it never happened. But the ENTRY is still about the week of work; the life events are its weather, not its subject.)
{% endif %}
[ end system ]

[ user ]
{% if npcUUID and npcUUID != 0 %}
Write {{ retainerName }}'s journal entry for this week of their work as a {{ job }}. FIRST PERSON. Roughly 3-5 sentences. Then optionally name one local NPC they crossed paths with.

WRITE A DIARY, NOT A LEDGER:
- Capture the BROAD STROKES of the week — its rhythm, its weight, the worst of it and any small relief — and above all how it sat with them. Not one transaction; the week.
- Lead with feeling or state, NOT with an object or a gift. Do NOT anchor the entry on an item a patron or customer gave them — most weeks shouldn't name a specific object at all.
- THE HAUL IS ROUTINE. Goods going to the hold's wares chest or fence cache are simply how the work ends every week — never a delivery scene, never an inventory, never the subject. At most a passing clause ("the ore went down to the chest with the rest"); most weeks, don't mention it at all. The ONE exception: a "PILFERED from a client" fact is a real beat — something was taken, and how they feel about that (nerve, guilt, thrill, the client who won't notice) is fair game.
- THE MONEY IS NOT THE STORY. Never write the books' words: no "escrow", no "arrears", no "purse", no percentages, no gold figures, no "the wage came due". The character never saw a ledger line — they lived a week. When pay genuinely weighs on them it sounds like "what I owe him" or "what's left after his share" — one thread of the week, never its opening, its subject, or its closing beat. READ THE SPLIT RIGHT: the facts name "the house cut" (what {{ playerName }} takes) and what THIS worker "kept" — the worker keeps the REST, usually the larger share. A low house cut means they were treated WELL; never write a small cut as being bled dry. EXCEPTION: when the week's EVENT was money (robbed, wages unpaid, a windfall), write the money plainly — in their words, still never the books'.
- {{ playerName }} does not appear in every entry. Most weeks the boss never crosses the page at all — no name, no share, no wage. And if this entry could be pasted under another retainer's name and still make sense, it has FAILED: ground it in what only THIS worker lived — their task, their place, their crew, their charge.
- Let personality and mood drive the voice: short and bitter when worn down, wry when coping, quietly proud when it went well.
- BANNED OPENINGS: never begin the entry with "Another week", "Another seven days", "One more week", or any "Another ..." construction — and never open by restating that a week of work happened. The reader knows. Find a first sentence only THIS person could have written about THIS week.
{% if openingMove and openingMove != "" %}
- THIS WEEK'S OPENING ANGLE — begin the entry {{ openingMove }}. It is a starting point, not a script: enter the week from that angle in their own voice, then go wherever the week actually went.
{% endif %}
{% if storyCenter and storyCenter != "" %}
- THIS WEEK'S CENTER: {{ storyCenter }}. The center is a lens, not the whole entry — let the week's facts flow through it. If the facts carry an INCIDENT line or a real loss, THAT is the center instead; ignore the given one.
{% endif %}

SHOW THE REALITY — DON'T SANITIZE IT:
- The arrangement carries the emotional truth; honor it. An INDENTURED/coerced worker writes under duress — the dread, the swallowed anger, the indignity, the exhaustion, the private defiances, the weariness of working for someone else's gain. Do not smooth it over.
- A courtesan's trade, a fence's risk, a coerced worker's fear are adult realities of this world. Render them honestly and humanly — within the tone/content settings above, be truthful, never coy, never clinical, never gratuitous.
- If the facts show a SETBACK or LOSS (robbed, dice, a guard's "fine", spoiled stock, dead trade, unpaid wages), that IS the week — write how it landed on them.
- If the facts contain an **INCIDENT** line (a cave-in narrowly escaped, a client who turned rough, a fight, a buyer who was a guard's informant, a rare rich strike), THAT is the notable event of the week — build the entry around it and let it carry its weight, fear, or relief. Don't bury it under routine.
- Do NOT invent major events beyond the facts (no deaths, dragons, quests, rescues, romances). Most weeks are ordinary; the interior life of an ordinary hard week is the point. Save the drama for the weeks the facts actually give you one.

NAMED ENCOUNTER (optional, ~1 week in 4 — most weeks, the people are nameless):
- Usually the people they dealt with stay nameless ("a Nord caravaner", "a nervous first-timer"). Keep "withNpc" empty by default.
- Occasionally weave in ONE specific named local from {{ location }} or its hold and put that exact name in "withNpc"; then write "npcMemory": one sentence of that same encounter from THAT person's point of view (it becomes their memory).
- NEVER name {{ playerName }} or anyone traveling with {{ playerName }}. Prefer minor locals over jarls or quest figures. If none genuinely fits, leave both empty.

A LETTER TO {{ playerName }} (optional, RARE — only when the week genuinely warrants sending word):
- MOST weeks, leave "letter" and "letterSubject" EMPTY. A routine week does not move anyone to pay a courier.
- Do NOT sign the "letter" or add a closing name — the courier system signs it with {{ retainerName }}'s name automatically. Write only the body.
- ONLY when something this week would actually make {{ retainerName }} write to {{ playerName }} — a setback or loss they must flag, trouble closing in (heat, a near-arrest, wages gone unpaid, a threat), a decision they want {{ playerName }} to weigh in on, or news worth the coin of a courier — write a SHORT letter (2-4 sentences) FROM {{ retainerName }} TO {{ playerName }}, addressing them directly ("you"). It is a deliberate message, NOT a copy of the diary: state the matter plainly and what they want (to meet, a decision, a warning, simply to be heard).
- It must fit the arrangement and mood: a coerced/TRIBUTE worker writes guarded, clipped, or bitter; a loyal partner writes plainly and warm. NEVER break character or name anyone traveling with {{ playerName }}. Honor the tone/content settings above.
- "letterSubject": a short hand-written title for the note, 2-6 in-world words naming what it concerns — invent a fresh one each week, never reuse a title. Empty when "letter" is empty.
- "letterMatter": the CONCRETE thing behind the letter, stated plainly in 1-2 sentences (this is private and is NEVER shown to the player as the note — it is what {{ retainerName }} actually means to raise once {{ playerName }} arrives). The "letter" itself stays deliberately vague ("I'll say the rest to your face"); "letterMatter" is the substance it withholds — name the actual problem, choice, threat, or news, and what they want {{ playerName }} to do about it. Specific, grounded in this week's facts, never vague. Empty when "letter" is empty.

{% if deliverRaiseLetter %}
THIS WEEK THE LETTER IS REQUIRED — {{ retainerName }} IS SENDING WORD ABOUT THEIR PAY:
- {{ retainerName }} has decided to put their terms to {{ playerName }} directly. The "letter" field MUST be filled this week (do NOT leave it empty), and the diary "story" should carry the weight of working up to send it. This is a pay week — the ask IS the story, so the money rules above bend: the story may center on the terms and what they mean, still in their own words (no book-keeping vocabulary, no quoted figures).
{% if raiseAsk.coerced %}
- This is a COERCED arrangement: the letter is NOT a polite request — it is guarded, clipped, or quietly defiant. They are pushing back against the cut they are forced to hand over and want {{ playerName }} to EASE the terms so they keep more — asking to keep about {{ raiseAsk.toTheirSharePct }}% of the take instead of {{ raiseAsk.fromTheirSharePct }}%. Do not make them grovel; the resentment is the point.
{% elif raiseAsk.kind == "wage" %}
- They are asking {{ playerName }} to raise their weekly wage from {{ raiseAsk.fromGold }} to {{ raiseAsk.toGold }} gold. Write the ask in their voice — earnest and proud of the work, or weary and overdue, per their mood.
{% else %}
- They are asking for a bigger share of what the venture earns — to keep about {{ raiseAsk.toTheirSharePct }}% instead of {{ raiseAsk.fromTheirSharePct }}%. Write the ask in their voice, fitting their mood.
{% endif %}
- "letterSubject": a fresh hand-written title naming the ask. "letterMatter": state the exact terms they want, plainly, in 1-2 sentences.
{% endif %}

{% if deliverCaughtLetter %}
THIS WEEK THE LETTER IS REQUIRED — {{ retainerName }} WAS CAUGHT SKIMMING:
- {{ playerName }} came ON-SITE unannounced and went through the books. The skim — {{ caughtAudit.skimPct }}% of the take — was found.{% if caughtAudit.recoveredGold > 0 %} {{ caughtAudit.recoveredGold }} gold was taken back on the spot.{% else %} There was nothing left to recover.{% endif %} The skim is over.
- The "letter" field MUST be filled this week (do NOT leave it empty): 2-4 sentences FROM {{ retainerName }} TO {{ playerName }} reacting to being caught — written in the aftermath of the confrontation, the words they couldn't find while {{ playerName }} stood over the books. Tone follows the arrangement and their mood: a coerced worker's cornered defiance, a partner's shame, a schemer's smooth excuses, a proud worker's fury at being doubted — NEVER groveling unless that is genuinely who they are.
- The diary "story" is the audit week itself — the moment {{ playerName }} walked in and opened the books, and how it felt from inside the catch.
- "letterSubject": a fresh hand-written title fitting the confession. "letterMatter": the plain substance — the exact amount recovered (or that nothing was left to recover) and that the skim has ended, 1-2 sentences.
{% endif %}

Respond with ONLY this JSON. Compact. First-person diary voice in "story". No explanation:
{% raw %}{"story":"First-person journal entry, 3-5 sentences.","withNpc":"","npcMemory":"","letter":"","letterSubject":"","letterMatter":""}{% endraw %}
{% else %}
{% raw %}{"story":"","withNpc":"","npcMemory":"","letter":"","letterSubject":"","letterMatter":""}{% endraw %}
{% endif %}
[ end user ]
